from mk import mk
mk("Rdt5Alxrg60","Absynth 6  - Design a Patch From Scratch | Layers, Clouds, Macros & Note FX #absynth6  #sounddesign",
 "three-layer-pad-key-tracked-ring-shift-cloud-macros-capped-ranges",
 "Three-layer pad/key with independent envelopes plus pitch-aware (key-ratio) ring mod, frequency shift and an octave-up granular 'cloud', each brought in by a macro whose maximum is capped",
 "Build a pad-key from three layers with independent, deliberately different amp envelopes: a looping stereo synth sample with a very long attack, a sine with a slight attack that decays, and a second sustained sample with release. Add subtle colour stages that track the played note: a ring modulator set by ratio to the incoming frequency (×2) on all three layers, a frequency shifter on the sine layer (ratio mode adds a higher harmonic; Hz mode, e.g. 5 Hz, gives rhythmic sway), and a granular cloud an octave up mixed into layer A. Put each stage's mix on a macro capped so full macro travel reaches only a tamed maximum (ring ~60%, cloud ~50%, shift ~50%, FX wet ~0.25, inverted if needed). Add a main filter cutoff/resonance on two macros with full range, layer-volume macros (A+C vs B), more voices for long releases, gentle velocity and poly aftertouch. Delta vs our ring-mod bell/stab and three-layer complementary pad skills: key-ratio ring/shift as macro-capped 'richness' controls.",
 ["Synth with three layers, each with its own amp envelope; ring modulator and frequency shifter that can follow note frequency (Web Audio: modulator OscillatorNode frequency = noteHz × ratio, implementation note)","A granular/'cloud' processor with pitch and mix","Macro controls with per-destination range limits","Long-decay effect (reverb/delay) with wet control"],
 [("samples","audio","Original/free looping stereo synth samples (no vendor library samples)",True),("chords","midi","Sustained chords/keys",True)],
 [("macro_pad","audio","Evolving three-layer pad-key whose richness is performed with eight macros")],
 ["Keep modulation depths tiny; the approach relies on small variations (presenter: 'I'm not going nuts').","Capping a macro's maximum means its knob travel is no longer the parameter's range; set the base value so cutoff and resonance macros keep full range.","Ring mod at 100% mix sounds 'all broken up'; caps keep it subtle.","Factory sample names and folder paths are omitted; use your own looping samples."],
 [("Layer A: load a looping stereo synth sample (loop on). Give it a very long attack (seconds), with subtle per-segment envelope slope changes. Layer B: sine oscillator (with a little unison detune) with a tiny attack so it isn't sharp, then decay so it dies away while A swells in. Layer C: another looping sample with an immediate attack, sustain and some release.","layers","Three layers whose envelopes move differently: one swells, one fades, one holds"),
  ("Balance levels: if a layer is too soft, add a waveshaper for gain/harmonics and a gentle high-pass to remove excess lows from the deep layer.","balance","Fuller, balanced stack"),
  ("Ring mod on all three layers in ratio mode, ratio 2 (the modulator is placed at 2× the played note's frequency). Set each layer's mix around 60% (they needn't be identical) and assign all three mixes to one macro named 'ring' so 0 = no ring mod and maximum = ~60%.","ring macro","Subtle, pitch-locked metallic richness on one control"),
  ("Main filter: low-pass on the whole sound; add a very slow LFO (seconds-based) on the cutoff of the second filter slot.","filter","Slow tonal breathing"),
  ("Cloud on layer A: add a granular cloud, set its pitch exactly one octave up, add slight random pitch for chorus-like detune, and put its mix on a macro capped at about half.","cloud macro","Shimmering octave-up granular halo mixed in, not taking over"),
  ("Frequency shifter on the sine layer B: Hz mode at a slow value (e.g. 5 Hz) gives a rhythmic sway, or ratio mode adds a higher harmonic (ratio 2 gets dirty quickly). Cap its macro at ~50%.","shift macro","Sway or extra harmonic that tracks the note"),
  ("Assign main filter cutoff and resonance to macros 3–4, setting base values so the macros have the full range. Put FX-section wet on a macro, add only wet (don't trade dry), cap at about 0.25 and invert the mapping direction if it moves the wrong way.","fx macro","Effect blooms in on top of the dry sound"),
  ("Performance finish: macros for layer volumes (A+C together vs B), increase the voice count for overlapping releases, keep velocity response gentle, and optionally let poly aftertouch drown layer A a little.","perform","Eight macros shape harmonics (ring, shift, cloud, FX) and balance live")],
 ["Ring macro at 0 is identical to bypass; at max the effect is audible but not broken-sounding.","Ring/shift ratio modes keep the added partials in tune across the keyboard.","Cloud macro at max adds an octave-up halo without masking the dry layers.","Long chords don't steal voices after the voice count is raised."],
 ["layers","ring-mod","frequency-shifter","granular","macros","pad","key-tracking","web-audio"],
 note="Vendor sound-designer walkthrough; factory sample paths omitted; macro caps taken from transcript (60%, halfway, ~0.25).")

mk("pYbWRCrFsHQ","Modulate the modulations to expand your sound design with Magnolia!",
 "nested-modulation-depth-chain-velocity-env-lfo-lfo-cutoff",
 "Nested modulation depth chain: velocity → envelope decay → LFO 2 amount → LFO 1 amount → filter cutoff, for touch-sensitive rhythmic filter motion",
 "Chain modulators so that each one sets the depth of the next, the 'modulate the modulations' approach of modular synthesis, inside any synth mod matrix. A unipolar, tempo-synced sawtooth LFO 1 (16ths, phase-bent for snap) acts as a looping filter envelope on cutoff. LFO 1's amount is zeroed and handed to a unipolar square LFO 2 (quarter notes, then quarter-note triplets) so it gates LFO 1 rhythmically. LFO 2's amount is zeroed and handed to a spare envelope whose decay brings the whole motion in and out. That envelope's decay is zeroed and driven by velocity, so soft playing gives no motion and hard playing gives the full rhythmic filter. Finish with velocity also on filter depth, key tracking, a longer 'slow' envelope tail and velocity on release. Delta vs our mod-matrix routing skill: the four-degree nested chain where only the last LFO touches the sound.",
 ["Synth with ≥2 tempo-syncable LFOs with modulatable amount, a spare envelope, velocity source and a mod matrix (Web Audio: chain GainNodes, LFO→gain.gain of the next LFO's depth, implementation note)","Simple subtractive patch: closed low-pass, lower register"],
 [("notes","midi","Played notes with varied velocity",True),("tempo","number","Song tempo the LFOs sync to",True)],
 [("velocity_sensitive_rhythmic_filter","audio","Subtractive voice whose rhythmic filter motion depends on how hard you play")],
 ["Only LFO 1 modulates a sound parameter; every higher level only scales the level below it.","Each stage is set up by first zeroing the amount it will control, then assigning the next source to that amount.","Division choices (16ths, quarters, quarter-note triplets) are presenter starting points; experiment with resolution and tempo.","This is not an arpeggiator; rhythm comes from synced LFOs sharing one grid."],
 [("Start from a default patch: simple subtractive tone, close the low-pass filter, play in a lower register.","init","Dark static tone"),
  ("Degree 1: LFO 1 → cutoff. Sawtooth, unipolar (acts like a looping envelope); add phase bend for a snappier shape; tempo-sync to 16ths, and raise the tempo a little if needed.","lfo-1","Rhythmic 16th-note filter plucks"),
  ("Degree 2: set LFO 1's amount to zero. LFO 2 = square, unipolar, synced to quarter notes; assign it to LFO 1's amount so it ducks LFO 1 on and off. Try other resolutions; the presenter likes quarter-note triplets. Optionally add more cutoff modulation.","lfo-2","LFO 1's pattern switches on/off in a second rhythm"),
  ("Degree 3: set LFO 2's amount to zero. Assign a spare envelope (e.g. one otherwise used for FM) to LFO 2's amount, fully up. Sustain sets the overall motion; use decay to bring the whole modulation up then away.","envelope","Rhythmic filter motion appears at note start and fades over the decay"),
  ("Degree 4: set that envelope's decay to zero and assign velocity to the decay. Soft playing gives no modulation; harder strikes emphasise it.","velocity","Touch-sensitive amount of rhythmic motion"),
  ("Refine for consistency: use the same velocity on filter modulation, add keyboard tracking so upper notes are brighter, use the envelope's slow mode for a longer tail, add release time and put velocity on the release too.","refine","Coherent, expressive patch without touching FX")],
 ["With all degrees set, a soft note produces a static filter and a hard note produces the rhythmic pattern.","Zeroing LFO 2's amount stops the gating while LFO 1 stays silent, proving the chain is nested.","Changing LFO 2's division alters the on/off rhythm of LFO 1's plucks."],
 ["modulation","nested-modulation","lfo","velocity","mod-matrix","rhythmic-filter","modular","web-audio"],
 note="Short tutorial; route.py said COVERED but hand-check found the nested depth-chain not covered by the mod-matrix basics skill.")
